    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that's a pretty detailed breakdown, thanks. so if the legal and philosophical arguments are that solid, why is the reparations movement still so dominant in the public conversation? like, if there are these clear constitutional and liability-based counter-arguments, is the push for reparations mostly just based on moral emotion rather than actual legal feasibility or logic?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While legal and constitutional hurdles are real, the dominance of the argument stems largely from a fundamental disagreement on the nature of justice itself—specifically, whether history should be settled at all or if moral debt can persist across generations. Proponents argue that contemporary inequality is the direct, unbroken legacy of the slave trade (continuing the \"slavery caste\" metaphor), effectively framing the issue as one of justice rather than legal liability. This moral narrative is so powerful that it often overshadows legal technicalities in public discourse, creating a scenario where a \"right\" to be unjust wrong is prioritized over strict legal precedents like the 13th Amendment.",
